## Version 3: Merge Pruning into the Manual Loop

### Phase 1: Context Checks
- [ ] Confirm v1.3's manual loop script runs cleanly on its own before modifying it
- [ ] Confirm v2's `prune()` function is tested and stable in isolation
- [ ] Map how `prune()`'s output (pruned image) will be passed into v1's loop without breaking existing logic
- [ ] Back up the working v1 script before editing (keep it as the Phase 5 control group)

### Phase 2: Pruned-Image Integration
- [ ] Replace the raw screenshot call in v1.3's loop with `prune()`'s output
- [ ] Confirm the loop still runs end-to-end using pruned images instead of raw ones
- [ ] Verify Gemini still receives a valid image object/format after pruning

### Phase 3: Decision Quality Comparison
- [ ] Run the same task with raw screenshots (v1 baseline) and pruned screenshots (v3) back to back
- [ ] Log whether the VLM's chosen actions differ between the two runs
- [ ] Flag any cases where pruning causes the VLM to miss an element or hallucinate one
- [ ] Record this comparison log — it's early evidence for the Phase 5 report

### Phase 4: Coordinate Mapping
- [ ] Capture each stitched block's position in the pruned image during the crop/stitch step (v2.3)
- [ ] Build a naive dictionary mapping pruned-image coordinates back to original screenshot bounding boxes
- [ ] Wire this mapping into the Actor step so a pruned-image click resolves to the correct real-browser click

### Phase 5: Validation Testing
- [ ] Run the full pruned-image loop on a simple task (e.g., land on a search results page)
- [ ] Confirm clicks on the pruned image correctly land on the intended element in the real browser, at least most of the time
- [ ] Run a regression check against the v1 control script to confirm no crashes or broken state
- [ ] Document known failure cases of the coordinate mapping for the v6 semantic-action upgrade to address later